Bot Protection is a security mechanism that detects and blocks automated programs (bots)
from abusing your website, mobile app, or API — while still allowing:
  - Legitimate users (humans)
  - Trusted/good bots (e.g. Googlebot / search crawlers)

Key idea: not all bots are bad. We block abusive bots, not every automated request.


Why do we need Bot Protection?

Without it, attackers can automate abuse at scale:

1) Brute-force login
   - Try thousands of passwords against one account

2) Credential stuffing
   - Use leaked username/password pairs from other sites

3) Web scraping
   - Steal product data, prices, content

4) Spam
   - Fake signups, fake comments, fake reviews

5) Scalping
   - Auto-buy limited tickets / products before real users

6) DDoS / HTTP flood
   - Flood your APIs with huge traffic to slow or crash the app


How Bot Protection Works

Usually layered — one technique alone is not enough:

1) Rate Limiting
   - Cap how many requests an IP / user / API key can make
   - Example: max 5 login attempts per minute per IP
   - First defense for brute force and floods

2) CAPTCHA
   - Challenge when traffic looks suspicious
   - Examples: Google reCAPTCHA, Cloudflare Turnstile
   - Goal: prove "a human is present"

3) Behavior Analysis
   - Spot non-human patterns:
     - Extremely fast clicks
     - Same request repeated nonstop
     - No mouse / scroll / natural navigation
   - Good for detecting scripted clients

4) Device / Browser Fingerprinting
   - Build a signature from browser/OS/device traits
   - Helps detect same bot hiding behind many IPs

5) IP Reputation
   - Block / score IPs already known as malicious
     (proxy farms, botnet ranges, previous attackers)

6) WAF (Web Application Firewall)
   - Sits in front of the app (Cloudflare, AWS WAF, NGINX)
   - Filters bad requests BEFORE they hit Spring Boot
   - Blocks common attack patterns + bot signatures

7) Machine Learning
   - Learns "normal" traffic
   - Flags anomalies automatically (advanced / enterprise)


Simple Example (Login API)

API: POST /api/login

Normal user:
  - 1 attempt every few minutes

Bot:
  - 500 attempts in 30 seconds

What bot protection can do:
  - Throttle / temporarily slow the IP
  - Require CAPTCHA
  - Block the IP
  - Alert ops / security team


Good bots vs Bad bots (interview nuance)

Good bots: search crawlers, monitoring health checks (often allowlisted)
Bad bots: credential stuffers, scrapers, spammers, scalpers

Interview tip: say "we allow trusted bots and block abusive automation"


Bot Protection in Spring Boot

Important: Spring Security alone is NOT bot protection.
Spring Security = authentication + authorization (who is the user / what can they do).
Bots can still hammer /login even with Spring Security enabled.

Typical real setup = EDGE + APP:

1) Edge / infra (best first line)
   - Cloudflare / AWS WAF / NGINX
   - Rate limit + bot challenge + IP reputation before traffic reaches your service

2) Application layer (Spring Boot)
   - Rate limiting with Bucket4j (token-bucket style limits on APIs)
   - CAPTCHA on sensitive endpoints (login, signup, password reset)
   - Spring Security for authn/authz (locks, password policy, etc.)
   - Monitoring: Prometheus / ELK to spot unusual spikes

Note on libraries:
  - Bucket4j → good for rate limiting (bot / abuse control)
  - Resilience4j → circuit breaker / retry / bulkhead (resilience, NOT bot protection)


Rate Limiting vs Circuit Breaker (common interview mix-up)

They solve DIFFERENT problems:

1) Rate limiting → SECURITY / abuse control
   - Goal: stop bots from flooding login
   - Best place: login service (fine-grained: per IP / username / device)
   - Also useful at gateway (coarse limit for all traffic)
   - Example: max 5 login attempts / minute / IP

2) Circuit breaker → RESILIENCE / failure control
   - Goal: if login service is down or slow, gateway stops calling it
   - Best place: API Gateway (or any caller of a downstream service)
   - Protects the WHOLE system from cascading failure
   - Does NOT detect bots — it reacts to errors / timeouts / high failure rate

So the common design is correct:

  Gateway  → circuit breaker (+ optional coarse rate limit)
  Login    → strict rate limit (+ CAPTCHA + account lockout)

Why rate limit ON the login service?
  - Gateway may not know username / business rules deeply
  - Login owns auth logic → best place for "5 fails then lock / CAPTCHA"
  - Even if someone bypasses gateway, service still protects itself

Why circuit breaker ON the gateway?
  - Gateway calls many services
  - If login is unhealthy → open circuit → fail fast → don't pile up threads
  - Protects gateway + other services from login outage


Recommended production mental model

Internet → WAF / CDN → API Gateway → Login Service
              ↑              ↑              ↑
         bot challenge   circuit breaker   rate limit (Bucket4j)
         + IP reputation + coarse rate     + CAPTCHA
                           limit           + Spring Security lockout


Interview Answer (30 seconds)

Bot protection prevents automated abuse of web apps and APIs while allowing real users
and trusted crawlers. It stops attacks like brute-force login, credential stuffing,
scraping, spam, scalping, and HTTP floods. We usually combine rate limiting, CAPTCHA,
IP reputation, fingerprinting, behavior analysis, and a WAF. In Spring Boot, put
strict rate limits + CAPTCHA on the login service; use a circuit breaker on the
gateway for resilience when login is failing — that is not bot protection, it is
failure isolation.
